WEBVTT

00:00:00.222 --> 00:00:04.975
FP32 uses 32-bit floating-point values and has been a common training and reference

00:00:04.975 --> 00:00:05.493
precision.

00:00:05.493 --> 00:00:07.310
FP16 uses half as many bits,

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:11.481
reducing memory and taking advantage of supported accelerator operations.

00:00:11.481 --> 00:00:14.730
BF16 also uses 16 bits but allocates them differently,

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:18.630
preserving a broad numerical range that is useful in training and some inference

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.510
workflows.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.781
Hardware support determines whether a precision provides an actual speed benefit.

00:00:22.781 --> 00:00:27.508
INT8 represents quantized values with 8-bit integers, often with per-tensor, per-channel,

00:00:27.508 --> 00:00:30.083
or grouped scale information.

00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:31.760
Four-bit and lower-bit weight formats reduce storage further,

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.740
but the nominal bit count does not include every scale, lookup, alignment,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:36.880
or metadata cost.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.885
Actual file and loaded sizes are therefore larger than parameters multiplied by bits

00:00:40.885 --> 00:00:41.590
alone.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:43.430
Weights are the learned model parameters.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.309
Activations are intermediate values produced while the model processes input.

00:00:46.309 --> 00:00:50.635
A system may store weights at low precision while computing activations or selected layers

00:00:50.635 --> 00:00:52.560
at higher precision to preserve quality.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.140
The key-value cache has its own format and memory cost,

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.380
which grows with context length and active sequences.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.675
Precision affects more than size.

00:00:59.675 --> 00:01:02.830
It changes numerical resolution, supported kernels, memory traffic,

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:04.710
and sometimes output behavior.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:08.200
Lower is not automatically faster if the hardware or runtime lacks an efficient

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.120
implementation.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.570
Treat labels such as 4-bit as a starting description,

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:15.570
then inspect the quantization method, runtime support, measured memory, speed,

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:16.360
and task quality.
